<article class="env-lemma" id="0D5T">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0D5T">Lemma <span data-tag="0D5T">20.28.2</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be morphisms of ringed spaces. Then $Rg_* \circ Rf_* = R(g \circ f)_*$ as functors $D(\mathcal{O}_ X) \to D(\mathcal{O}_ Z)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/079W" data-tag="079W">20.28.1</a> we see that $Rg_* \circ Rf_*$ is adjoint to $Lf^* \circ Lg^*$. We have $Lf^* \circ Lg^* = L(g \circ f)^*$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0D5S" data-tag="0D5S">20.27.2</a> and hence by uniqueness of adjoint functors we have $Rg_* \circ Rf_* = R(g \circ f)_*$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>